.SH DESCRIPTION
Cost tools such as
.BR ccrepo (1)
label usage by the working directory a session was started in.
A subagent started from inside another session runs in its parent's directory,
so work done for one repo by a subagent of another is billed to the parent's
repo, not the repo it was for.
The fix is structural: each repo's work runs as its own
.B claude \-p
session, started inside a worktree of that repo whose last path component is
the repo's name.
Doing that by hand for a dozen repos, without taking a laptop past its limits,
is what
.B ccfleet
is for.
.PP
It makes the worktree, renders a prompt template into the session's prompt,
launches the session under
.BR nice (1),
reads the session's own verdict from its output, writes that to a result file,
and puts the worktree away.
The
.B drive
and
.B drain
commands do this for many repos in turn, starting a new session only while the
machine's load, free memory and free disk all have room.
.PP
.B ccfleet
holds no estate knowledge of its own.
The repo list, the model tier of each repo, notes about each, the review queue,
the principal's words and the budget's reset time all come from a JSON config
that the adopter keeps (see
.BR CONFIG ).
A fictional example ships as
.IR instruments/ccfleet.example.json .
Keep the real one outside any public repo: it names repos and can carry
the principal's own words.
.PP
It is Python, not shell, on purpose.
A shell reads a script as it runs, so editing a driver mid-run broke every
running job's tail.
Python compiles the whole file at start, so a running driver is immune;
a job started after an edit runs the edited copy.
.SS What every launch does
These are fixed, because each was learned from a real run.
.IP \(bu 2
.B claude \-p
is started with standard input from
.IR /dev/null .
Otherwise a "no stdin" warning lands in the JSON output and the result cannot
be read.
.IP \(bu 2
Every launch names its model with
.BR \-\-model .
A launch with none inherits the default tier without saying so.
The model comes from, in order,
.BR \-\-model ,
a job's
.B MODEL=
field, the repo's
.B model
in the config, and the config's
.BR default_model .
A pass (see
.BR pass )
uses
.B pass_model
first.
.IP \(bu 2
A new session is given its id before it starts
.RB ( \-\-session\-id ) ,
so the
.B START
line, the claim and the result file carry it even when the session dies
before printing anything.
.IP \(bu 2
A session still running after
.B session_timeout_minutes
(default 480) is ended, with every process it started, and reads as
.BR TIMEOUT .
.IP \(bu 2
The worktree is
.IR <worktrees_root>/<label>-<HHMM>/<repo> ,
with the time in UTC.
Its basename is the repo, because that basename is the billing key.
.IP \(bu 2
The session runs with the
.B nice
value the config sets, so a busy fleet does not starve the machine's own work.
.IP \(bu 2
The session's environment always sets
.BR CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS=0 ,
over the config's
.B env
too.
Without it
.B claude \-p
kills a background subagent about ten minutes after the orchestrator's last
turn, so a reviewer still working on its second act died mid-act.
.IP \(bu 2
The config is the whole grant.
Every launch passes
.B \-\-setting\-sources project,local
so no user-level settings file loads, and
.B \-\-settings
with
.IR <kit_dir>/settings/<session>.json ,
a file ccfleet writes: the user-level settings less their allow rules,
default mode, extra directories and any hook that approves tool calls, with
the config's
.B settings
on top.
Their deny and ask rules, other hooks, plugins and environment are kept:
they restrict or equip a session, they do not widen what it may do.
Before this a config that left a tool out could not stop it, because a
headless session took the operator's own allow rules too.
Leaving the user source out also leaves out the user-level
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
so ccfleet appends that file to the system prompt
.RB ( \-\-append\-system\-prompt\-file ),
and the operator's standing instructions still bind.
A repo's own settings files still load; see
.BR THREATS .
.IP \(bu 2
A child that
.B drive
or
.B drain
starts runs in a session of its own, so closing the driver's terminal does
not end it or the
.B claude
it started.
.SS The private-name guard
A prompt bound for a public repo, or for one whose visibility is unknown, is
refused before any worktree is made if it contains a private name.
The private names are the config's
.B private_names
plus every repo the config marks private.
A name matches in any case wherever no letter or digit touches it on either
side, so a hyphen, underscore, dot or slash does not hide it.
That catches the forms ccfleet and its prompts build around a name: a
.I <label>-<HHMM>
worktree folder, a
.I name-kit
folder,
.IR name_notes ,
and
.IR atelier--name .
Visibility comes from the repo's
.B visibility
in the config, else from
.B gh repo view
when
.BR gh (1)
is installed; if neither answers, the repo is treated as public.
.PP
The guard is a literal match.
It catches the names listed and nothing that was forgotten.
.SS The headroom gate
A start may go only when every reading has room.
Load counts as held when the one-minute or five-minute average reaches
.BR gate.max_load .
Memory holds when the free percentage is below
.BR gate.min_mem_pct .
It reads
.BR memory_pressure (1)
on macOS and
.I /proc/meminfo
elsewhere, and an unreadable figure holds, unless
.B min_mem_pct
is 0.
Disk holds when the free space under
.B worktrees_root
is below
.B gate.min_disk_gb
gigabytes.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Validate the config and print what it resolves to, as JSON, without the
.B vars
values (their names are listed).
The output includes private names and repo notes: do not paste it anywhere
public.
.IP
When
.B claude_args
widens what a session may do unasked, a
.B PERMISSIONS:
line says so, and a bypass of every permission check says
.BR "PERMISSIONS: BYPASS" ;
see
.BR THREATS .
The config's
.B settings
permissions count as well.
.IP
A
.B GRANT:
line names what the user-level settings would have granted and is left
out, and what is kept from them.
Each user-level hook prints as
.B HOOK kept:
or
.B HOOK dropped:
with the reason: it approves tool calls, or a
.B drop_hooks
text matched it.
An
.B INSTRUCTIONS:
line says whether a user-level
.I CLAUDE.md
is appended, and
.B REPO SETTINGS:
that a repo's own settings files still load.
.IP
It then lists the placeholders of
.B pass_prompt
and of each
.B \-\-prompt
template that nothing fills: neither ccfleet, nor the config's
.BR vars ,
nor a
.BR \-\-set .
Any such name prints as
.B UNSET
and
.B check
exits 5, as the launch would.
A name only a pass or a resume fills is listed apart, not as unset.
.TP
.B gate
Print one headroom reading and whether a start may go now:
.B go
(exit 0) or
.B hold
(exit 1).
.TP
.BI run " repo"
Run one session for
.IR repo .
It checks that
.I repo
is a git checkout under
.BR repos_root ,
fetches it (unless
.BR \-\-dry\-run ),
makes the worktree, renders the template, launches, harvests the result, and
removes the worktree when the end was clean.
With a remote, the worktree is detached at the remote's default branch.
With none, it is a new branch
.I fleet/<label>-<HHMMSS>
off the configured mainline, deleted again with the worktree.
.IP
The worktree is removed only when the
.B RESULT
is clean (a word in
.BR queue.release_on ,
default
.B DONE
and
.BR SKIPPED )
and the tree holds no unlanded work: no uncommitted or untracked file,
no commit that is on no remote branch (with no remote, on no branch but
its own).
It is never removed by force.
Any other end keeps it and prints a
.B KEPT
line saying why, so a cut-off session's work is never lost.
A clean
.B RESULT
whose worktree still holds unlanded work is not clean: it reads as
.B UNLANDED
(see
.BR RESULTS ),
so no claim is released and the work stays tied to its item.
Only a worktree adopted with
.B \-\-in
and no
.B \-\-item
is not judged, since it is the caller's.
.IP
Before
.BR START ,
a
.B WIDENED
line names each grant the worktree's own
.I .claude/settings.json
or
.I .claude/settings.local.json
makes: those files load with the project, beyond the config.
.IP
The session is told how to land its work.
With a remote: pull with rebase from the default branch, then push to it
(unless the repo's own instructions require a pull request).
With none: rebase onto the mainline and fast-forward the primary checkout,
never pushing.
.TP
.BI drive " jobs"
Run a jobs file.
Each job is started as its own
.B ccfleet run
child, in file order, at most
.B \-\-max
at once, one start per
.B stagger_seconds
and only while the gate has room.
Every job's label must be its own: a jobs file whose labels repeat is
refused before anything starts, naming the label and its lines.
The label keys the job's result file, so a repeat would run one job and
skip the rest.
.IP
One drive writes all its results under one day,
.IR <kit_dir>/results/<day>/ ,
today (UTC) at its start unless
.B \-\-day
names another.
A job whose result file there records a clean
.B RESULT
is skipped, and so is one that ended
.B PARTIAL
or
.B BLOCKED
(settled: see
.BR RESULTS );
one that ended any other way runs again.
So running the same file again on the same day resumes it, and
.B \-\-day
resumes a drive begun before midnight.
.IP
Once
.B reset_utc
is reached a drive starts no further job, as a
.B drain
does; jobs already running finish.
.TP
.B drain
Keep review passes running from the adopter's own queue until the queue is
empty, the budget's reset arrives, the stop file appears, a pass ends with a
.B RESULT
in
.BR queue.stop_on ,
or three passes in a row end badly.
Passes already running are left to finish, but not forever: one running
past its time limit and a quarter hour more is logged as hung and is not
waited for.
It prints
.B DRAIN EXIT:
and the reason when it ends, naming any hung pass.
.IP
A reset already reached ends it before anything is called, with exit 3.
Otherwise it calls
.B queue.ranked
first (a dry drain does not), then picks.
A pick that ends before its session starts (a failed fetch, a brief not on
the branch, a guard) is taken back off the launched list and counts as a bad
end.
A pick whose claim the queue refuses is taken back off it too, but is not a
bad end, and it is kept out of
.B next
for the rest of the drain: the queue is handed the launched list plus those
picks, in a file beside it ending
.IR .skip ,
removed when the drain ends.
A pass whose launcher ended with no
.B END
line while its session still runs (the launcher was killed) is logged as
such, not as cut off; see
.BR pass .
A drain that started nothing exits 4 when the queue was empty, 3 when the
reset had come, and 1 when every pick ended before its session started;
one that started a pass exits 0.
.IP
A dry drain, before its rehearsed launches, prints the reading
.B plan
makes (the same function), each line prefixed
.BR "DRY plan:" .
With no
.B queue.evidence
it says so in one line, and a queue command that fails is said and the
rehearsal carries on.
It still never calls
.BR queue.ranked .
.TP
.B plan
Read the review queue and print what it holds, and what ccfleet's own past
passes cost.
It reads and never judges: ranking and routing are an agent's work, by
.BR "/atelier:review plan" ,
and the seal is the adopter's queue's.
It calls
.B queue.evidence
(and
.B queue.owed
when set) and none of
.BR ranked ,
.BR take ,
.BR next ,
.B release
or
.BR ended ,
writes no launched list, claim or worktree, and starts no session.
It is the one reading of the queue:
.B /atelier:review plan
runs it,
.B /atelier:review run
runs it at its start, and
.B drain \-\-dry\-run
prints it.
The output is a table of the evidence rows (repo, item, title, the routes it
fits), then the owed reviews that have no brief, then the cost history.
A queue command that fails, or prints something other than the JSON array
described under
.BR "THE QUEUE INTERFACE" ,
exits 1.
A config with no
.B queue.evidence
exits 64.
.IP
The cost history comes from
.I <kit_dir>/logs/pass\-*.out
and the
.B claude \-p
JSON log each names: for the passes a drain launched that ended clean, the
count, median and maximum of
.BR total_cost_usd ,
.B duration_ms
and
.BR num_turns ,
where the log has them.
Every pass ccfleet launches is a local session, so only the
.B local
route is measured.
Any other route a row's
.B fits
names, and
.B local
with no logs, read
.BR "no measurement" ;
nothing is estimated.
A pass run by hand leaves no
.I .out
file and is not counted.
.TP
.BI pass " repo item brief"
One drain pass; the command
.B drain
starts for each pick.
It needs
.BR pass_prompt ,
.B queue.take
and
.B queue.release
in the config; without them it is refused with exit 64 before any worktree
is made.
It makes the worktree, checks that the brief file exists in it (so the brief
has been pushed), claims the item through the queue's
.B take
command, runs the orchestrator session under
.BR pass_model ,
and, when its
.B RESULT
is in
.BR queue.release_on ,
releases the claim and removes the worktree.
A pass that ends any other way keeps both, so it can be resumed: an error
end, a time limit, a session killed with no
.B END
line, or a clean
.B RESULT
that left unlanded work, which reads as
.B UNLANDED
(see
.BR RESULTS ).
A release the queue refuses keeps the worktree and exits 2.
Either way the
.B queue.ended
command, if set, is called.
A review log an earlier pass left at this pass's path is moved to
.I <kit_dir>/review\-logs/withdrawn/
once the claim is taken, so a new reviewer never starts from an earlier
reviewer's account.
.IP
A SIGTERM or SIGHUP to a pass, a
.B run
or a
.B resume
leaves its session running, since its worktree may hold unsaved work: it
prints
.BR "LAUNCHER STOPPED" ,
keeps the claim and the worktree, and exits 128 plus the signal.
The session's process id stays in
.I <kit_dir>/sessions/
while it runs, so a
.B resume
waits for it and a
.B drain
names it.
A ^C ends the session with its launcher.
.TP
.BI resume " repo worktree session"
Finish a pass a usage limit cut off, in its own session:
.B claude \-p \-\-resume
.IR session ,
started in the kept worktree, with a fresh prompt from
.BR \-\-prompt ,
which gets every value a
.B run
gets (see
.BR TEMPLATES ).
The worktree must exist and be named for
.IR repo ,
as for
.BR "run \-\-in" .
Only one resume of a session runs at a time; a second is refused with exit 2.
So is a resume while the session itself still runs, its launcher killed by
a signal: wait for it to end.
With
.B \-\-item
it needs
.BR queue.release ,
else it is refused with exit 64 before the session starts.
When it ends with a
.B RESULT
in
.B queue.release_on
and
.B \-\-item
was given, the claim is released and the worktree removed; one that still
leaves unlanded work reads as
.B UNLANDED
and keeps both.
A release the queue refuses is printed, keeps the worktree and exits 2.
Without
.BR \-\-item ,
no queue command is called and the worktree is kept.
It starts nothing else.
.IP
Its verdict heads the pass's own result file, the newest
.I results/<day>/<repo>\-<item>.md
(today's when there is none;
.I resume\-<repo>\-<session>.md
without
.BR \-\-item ),
with the earlier end kept below it, so a count from the result files reads
the pass as the resume ended it.
In a repo with no remote, the landing line names the worktree's own branch,
which is deleted with the worktree.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Common
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
The JSON config.
Without it,
.B $CCFLEET_CONFIG
is used; with neither the command refuses with exit 64.
.TP
.BI \-\-by " id"
The orchestrating session's id, filled into prompts as
.BR {BY} .
Default:
.BR $CCFLEET_BY ,
else
.BR unknown .
.TP
.B \-\-doc
Print the long reference text kept in the source, and exit.
This page is the fuller and the better-kept reference.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a one-screen digest.
A command takes
.B \-\-help
for its own flags.
.SS Of check
.TP
.BI \-\-prompt " file"
Also list this template's unset placeholders; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-set " K=V"
A value a job will supply, so it is not listed as unset; repeatable.
.SS Of run, drive, drain, pass
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Do everything up to the launch, print what would start, and start nothing.
No worktree is made and nothing is fetched.
A dry
.B drive
or
.B drain
also skips the stagger and the gate.
.SS Of run
.TP
.BI \-\-prompt " file"
The prompt template.
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-label " name"
Names the worktree's parent folder, the log and the result file.
Characters outside letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen become hyphens.
Default: the repo; for a fold
.RB ( \-\-in
with
.BR \-\-item ),
.IR <repo>\-<item> .
.TP
.BI \-\-model " id"
The model for this launch.
Overrides every other source.
.TP
.BI \-\-set " K=V"
Fill
.B {K}
in the template; repeatable.
A value with no
.B =
is refused with exit 64.
A value written
.BI @file: path
takes that file's text, for a multi-line task.
.B MODEL=
sets the model, below
.BR \-\-model .
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Leave the worktree in place afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-\-in " worktree"
Adopt an existing worktree instead of making one, to fold earlier work.
Its basename must be the repo's name (a differently named directory would bill
some other label), and it is kept afterwards, because it is the caller's.
.TP
.BI \-\-item " item"
With
.BR \-\-in ,
for the fold that finishes a cut-off pass: on a clean
.B RESULT
release
.IR item 's
claim through
.B queue.release
(and call
.BR queue.ended ),
then remove the worktree unless it holds unlanded work.
Any other end keeps both.
The template also gets
.B {ITEM}
and the pass's own
.BR {REVIEW_LOG} ,
as for a
.BR resume .
Its result is written as a resume's is: into the pass's own result file,
headed by the fold's verdict, the earlier end kept below, so two folds in
one repo never share a file.
A refused release exits 2.
Without
.B \-\-in
it is refused with exit 64.
.SS Of plan
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object for an agent:
.BR evidence ,
.B owed
(null when
.B queue.owed
is not set),
.B history
and
.BR notes ,
instead of the table.
.TP
.B \-\-fetch
Have the queue read each repo's remote first: an argument written exactly
.B {FETCH}
in
.B queue.evidence
or
.B queue.owed
is filled with
.B \-\-fetch
when this is given and dropped when it is not.
An entry with no
.B {FETCH}
fetches nothing, and plan says so.
.SS Of drive and drain
.TP
.BI \-\-max " n"
Most sessions at once; 0 holds every start, and a negative number is refused
with exit 64.
Default: the config's
.BR max_concurrent .
.TP
.B \-\-serial\-per\-repo
Of
.BR drive :
never two jobs for one repo at once, so a repo has one writer.
.TP
.BI \-\-day " YYYY-MM-DD"
Of
.BR drive :
the results day whose folder the drive's jobs write to and whose clean
results it skips.
Default: today, UTC, at the drive's start.
.SH JOBS FILE
A tab-separated file for
.BR drive ,
one job a line: the repo, a label, the prompt template's path, then any number
of
.IB KEY = VALUE
fields that fill
.B {KEY}
in that template.
.B MODEL=
sets the job's model; a value written
.BI @file: path
takes that file's text.
A line that is blank or starts with
.B #
is skipped.
A line with fewer than three fields, a repo given as a path, or a label used
by more than one line is refused with exit 64, before anything starts.
A trailing field with no
.B =
is ignored.
.SH TEMPLATES
A prompt is a text file with
.B {UPPER_CASE}
placeholders.
These are always filled:
.TP
.B {REPO} {GH} {BR} {VIS}
The repo's name, its GitHub path (or its remote, or
.BR "(no remote)" ),
its mainline branch, and its visibility.
.TP
.B {WT} {PRIMARY} {SCRATCH}
The session's worktree, the primary checkout, and a scratch folder,
.IR <kit_dir>/scratch/<repo>/ .
.TP
.B {LANDING}
How to land work, as above.
.TP
.B {NOTES} {BY} {DATE} {SESSION}
The repo's notes (default
.BR none ),
the
.B \-\-by
id, today's UTC date, and the session's id (chosen before a new session
starts; the resumed one's for a resume).
.TP
.B {PIN}
The repo's atelier pin, when the template uses it and neither the config's
.B vars
nor the job supplies it.
It is read with
.I tools/pins.py
from the atelier named by
.BR vars.ATELIER ,
else the one this file lives in, run as
.B pins.py \-\-head \-\-child
on the primary checkout.
Failing that, it is the first
.BI atelier@ sha
in the repo's
.I CLAUDE.md
on the mainline.
Failing both, it is the word
.B UNRESOLVED
and the command prints why, so a prompt never carries an empty pin.
.PP
Then come the config's
.B vars
and the job's own fields.
A
.B pass
adds
.BR {ITEM} ,
.B {BRIEF}
and
.BR {REVIEW_LOG} ,
the path of the reviewer's private log,
.IR <kit_dir>/review\-logs/<repo>\-<item>\-<hash>.md ,
outside any repo; its folder is made, the file is not.
The short hash of the exact repo and item keeps two pairs that fold to the
same characters apart.
A
.B resume
adds
.BR {ITEM} ,
and with
.B \-\-item
the same
.B {REVIEW_LOG}
the pass had, so a revived reviewer finds its log (a log kept under the
earlier name, with no hash, is used where it exists).
.B "ccfleet check"
names the placeholders a template needs that nothing fills.
A placeholder left unfilled is a refusal (exit 5), never a silent gap.
.SH CONFIG
JSON.
Required:
.TP
.B kit_dir
Where logs, results, scratch folders, the stop file and the driver lock live.
.TP
.B repos_root
Primary checkouts are at
.IR <repos_root>/<repo> .
.TP
.B worktrees_root
Job worktrees go under here.
.TP
.B default_model
The model a job uses when neither it nor its repo names one.
.PP
Optional:
.TP
.B claude_bin
The executable.
Default:
.BR claude .
.TP
.B claude_args
Extra arguments for every launch, such as a permission mode.
A headless session has no one to ask, so it works with whatever permissions
these grant.
Choose them on purpose, the narrowest that does the work: see
.BR THREATS .
The example config sets none;
.B check
names any that widen what a session may do.
They may not hold
.BR \-\-setting\-sources ,
.BR \-\-settings ,
.B \-\-append\-system\-prompt
or
.BR \-\-append\-system\-prompt\-file :
ccfleet sets those so the config is the whole grant.
.TP
.B settings
Claude Code settings every session gets, on top of the user-level settings
less their grants (see
.BR "What every launch does" ).
Objects merge key by key and lists join; any other value is the config's.
Its
.B permissions
are part of the grant, with
.BR claude_args ,
and
.B check
names them.
Default: none.
.TP
.B drop_hooks
A list of texts: a user-level hook entry with a command that holds one of
them is left out of every session.
A hook whose command prints an allow decision is left out without being
named, because it grants; one whose script answers allow cannot be seen
from its command line, so name it here.
Default: none.
.TP
.B session_timeout_minutes
How long one session may run before it is ended.
Default 480; 0 means no limit.
.TP
.B nice
The
.BR nice (1)
adjustment.
Default 5; 0 turns it off.
.TP
.B max_concurrent
Default for
.BR \-\-max .
Default 4.
.TP
.B stagger_seconds
The pause after each start, so memory and load can show it.
Default 90.
.TP
.B poll_seconds
How often the drivers look again.
Default 30.
.TP
.B gate
.IR max_load ,
.IR min_mem_pct
and
.IR min_disk_gb ;
defaults are twice the core count, 25 and 20.
.TP
.B reset_utc
.B YYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM
in UTC.
No new session starts at or after it; this is a budget's reset time.
A
.B resume
and a fold
.RB ( run
.B \-\-in
.I WT
.B \-\-item
.IR ITEM )
finish a pass that started before it, so the reset does not stop them:
they run after it on the principal's word only.
.TP
.B private_names
Names a public repo's prompt must never contain.
.TP
.B vars
Template variables for every job.
.TP
.B env
Extra environment variables for every session, as
.IR NAME : value .
.B check
prints their names, not their values.
.TP
.B repos
Per repo:
.BR model ,
.BR notes ,
.B visibility
(public or private),
.B github
(the path, when the remote does not give it) and, for a repo with no remote,
.BR branch
(default
.BR main ).
.PP
For
.B drain
and
.BR pass :
.TP
.B pass_prompt
The orchestrator prompt template, one per pass.
.TP
.B pass_model
The orchestrator's model.
The reviewer's own tier is the prompt's business.
.TP
.B queue
.BR next ,
.BR ranked ,
.B take
and
.BR release ,
optionally
.BR ended ,
.B release_on
and
.BR stop_on ;
for
.BR plan ,
.B evidence
and optionally
.BR owed :
see below.
.SH THE QUEUE INTERFACE
The review queue is the adopter's own tool; ccfleet only calls it.
Each entry is an argument list, with a leading
.B ~
expanded and
.B {NAME}
placeholders filled before the call.
An unknown placeholder is left as it is.
A command that cannot start, or runs past ten minutes, counts as a failed
call (exit 127 or 124), never a hang.
.TP
.B next
Prints
.I repo item brief
(the brief as a path inside the repo) for the next pass to run, or nothing
when none is launchable.
Only the last non-blank line of output is read.
A line with a tab is split on tabs, so a field may hold a space; a line with
none is split on white space.
.B {LAUNCHED}
is the path of a file listing what this drain has started (one
tab-separated
.I repo item brief
a line), so the queue can skip them, and
.B {BY}
is the orchestrator id.
A pick already on that list is taken as nothing launchable, so a queue that
ignores the list cannot start one pass over and over.
.TP
.B ranked
Exits 0 only when the whole queue, every repo's, has been ranked and routed
for this run; called once before the first pick, with
.BR {BY} .
Any other exit starts nothing: the drain prints
.B DRAIN EXIT: not ranked
and exits 1.
A drain config without it is refused.
.TP
.B take
Claims the pass.
Any nonzero exit means another session holds it, and the pass is not run.
It can use every prompt value, and
.BR {ITEM} ,
.BR {BRIEF} ,
.B {LOG}
and
.B {SESSION}
(the id the session will have), so the claim can record it before the
session starts.
.TP
.B release
Frees the claim after a pass ends with a
.B RESULT
in
.B release_on
(default
.B DONE
and
.BR SKIPPED )
and leaves no unlanded work;
a pass that ends any other way, or as
.BR UNLANDED ,
keeps its claim.
It can use
.BR {ITEM} ,
.BR {BRIEF} ,
.BR {SESSION} ,
.B {RESULT}
and
.BR {LOG} .
A nonzero exit is printed, keeps the worktree, and exits 2, for a
.BR pass ,
a
.B resume
and a
.BR "run \-\-item" .
.TP
.B ended
Optional.
Called after every pass, resume and
.BR "run \-\-item" ,
whatever its
.BR RESULT ,
with the same values as
.BR release ,
for instance to record the session, log and worktree on the claim.
A nonzero exit is printed and otherwise ignored.
.TP
.B evidence
For
.B plan
and a dry drain only.
Prints a JSON array, one object per briefed, unrun review, each with at least
.BR repo ,
.BR item ,
.B brief
and
.BR title ;
every other field is passed through untouched (an adopter's tool may print
.BR briefed ,
.BR his_words ,
.BR dates_ahead ,
.BR signals ,
.BR live_write ,
.B fits
(the routes the review suits, as a list of names) and
.BR lane ).
Read\-only: it must claim, seal and start nothing.
An argument written exactly
.B {FETCH}
is filled with
.B \-\-fetch
for
.B plan \-\-fetch
and dropped otherwise.
.TP
.B owed
Optional, for
.B plan
only.
Prints a JSON array of owed reviews that have no brief yet:
.BR repo ,
.B item
and
.BR title ,
and optionally
.BR why .
Read\-only;
.B {FETCH}
works here too.
Without it,
.B plan
prints owed as null and says so.
.TP
.B release_on
A list of
.B RESULT
words that make an end clean: the claim is released, the worktree removed,
and a
.B drive
skips the job.
In a drain they also reset the count of bad ends.
A clean word whose worktree holds unlanded work reads as
.B UNLANDED
instead, which this list may not hold.
Default:
.B DONE
and
.BR SKIPPED .
.TP
.B stop_on
A list of
.B RESULT
words that stop the drain.
Default:
.BR "BUDGET EXHAUSTED" .
.SH RESULTS
The session's output is read as JSON, tolerating a warning line before it.
An end is clean only when the session exited 0 and its JSON flags no error
.RB ( is_error ,
or a
.B subtype
other than
.BR success ).
Any other end reads as
.B ERROR
.RB ( TIMEOUT
for one ended at its time limit) whatever its text says, so a cut-off
session is never read as done; only a
.B queue.stop_on
word the session printed itself is kept, since stopping is safe.
.PP
The first line of the result text that begins with
.B RESULT:
(as in
.BR "RESULT: DONE" )
gives the verdict; a later one, or a mention inside another line, does not.
On a clean end it counts only beside a line starting
.B ITEM:
or
.B REPO:
(the final-output shape), but for a
.B queue.stop_on
word.
Bold or backticks around it are ignored.
A phrase in
.B queue.release_on
or
.B queue.stop_on
is read whole
.RB ( "BUDGET EXHAUSTED" );
otherwise the first capital-letter word is the verdict, so
.B RESULT: SKIPPED NEEDS LIVE WRITE
reads
.BR SKIPPED .
.B claude \-p
prints only the last turn.
A session whose last turn followed a background notification can print one
with no such line, though its report was written earlier.
Then the session's own transcript,
.IR <config>/projects/*/<session>.jsonl ,
is read, on a clean end only, where
.I <config>
is
.B $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
or
.IR ~/.claude .
The newest assistant text written since the launch that carries such a line
and a line starting
.B ITEM:
or
.B REPO:
(the final-output shape) gives the verdict; a line of narration that only
mentions a
.B RESULT
is never taken.
The result file then says it was read from the transcript.
No such line anywhere, or unreadable output, reads as
.BR UNKNOWN ;
output that is not the JSON log reads so even when it holds such a line,
but for a
.B queue.stop_on
word.
.PP
.B PARTIAL
and
.BR BLOCKED ,
the fleet prompts' own verdicts, are settled: not clean, so no claim is
released, but a
.B run
with no claim removes its worktree when it holds no unlanded work, and a
.B drive
does not start the job again.
.PP
A clean verdict (a word in
.BR queue.release_on )
whose worktree still holds uncommitted or untracked files, or commits on no
remote branch, reads as
.BR UNLANDED ,
and an
.B UNLANDED
line before
.B END
says what was left.
The result file starts by saying so.
It is not clean: no release, the worktree is kept, a drive runs the job
again, a drain counts a bad end, and
.B resume
can finish the pass.
A
.B DONE
that left uncommitted work once released its claim, so the work was tied to
no queue item.
.B UNLANDED
may not be in
.BR queue.release_on .
A run past a usage limit ends with no such line, which is how a spent budget
shows up from outside.
.PP
The command prints
.B START
and
.B END
lines (the
.B END
carries the exit code, the verdict, the session id and the result path).
The result text is written to
.IR <kit_dir>/results/<date>/<label>.md ,
with the session id, exit code and verdict in a comment on its first line.
A resume and a fold write their pass's file instead; see
.BR resume .
.PP
The
.B drain
reads a pass's last
.B END
line.
A verdict in
.B queue.stop_on
stops it; one in
.B queue.release_on
resets its count of bad ends;
anything else counts one bad end, and three in a row stop it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/logs/<label>-<HHMM>.json
A session's raw output; a
.I .err
file beside it holds its standard error.
An adopted worktree adds
.I \-in\-<HHMMSS>
to the name.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/logs/run\-<label>.out, pass\-*.out
What each child
.B ccfleet
printed, for a drive or a drain.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/results/<date>/
One result file per label, per UTC day.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/scratch/<repo>/
A folder for the session's own working files.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/review\-logs/
Each pass's private review log while it runs; the pass's own fold moves it
into the review and deletes it.
After a drain the folder holds, beside
.IR withdrawn/ ,
only the logs of passes whose claim is still held (cut off, refused
release), kept for their resume.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/review\-logs/withdrawn/
Logs an earlier pass left behind, moved here, dated, when a new pass of the
item takes its claim (and where the review run skill moves a withdrawn
pass's log by hand).
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/children.tsv
The children a driver started, so a later driver counts those still running
against its ceiling, even after the driver that started them died.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/settings/
Each session's settings file and, when there is a user-level
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
its copy for the system prompt; owner-only, kept for reading back what a
session was given.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/locks/
One lock per session being resumed.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/sessions/<session>.pid
The process id of a session while it may run, written by its launcher and
removed when the launcher sees it end.
One left by a killed launcher stays while the session runs; one whose
process is gone is removed when next read.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/driver.pid, driver.log
The driver lock (held with
.BR flock (2)
while the driver lives) and the driver's log, in UTC.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/launched\-reset\-<reset>.txt
What a drain has started for the budget ending at
.B reset_utc
(written without its colon), so a drain that crosses midnight keeps one list.
With no
.BR reset_utc ,
.I launched\-<date>.txt
per UTC day.
A dry drain keeps its own,
.IR launched\-dry.txt ,
removed when it ends, so a rehearsal never empties the real queue.
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/max
The live ceiling while a driver runs; see
.BR STOPPING .
.TP
.I <kit_dir>/stop
See
.BR "STOPPING" .
.SH STOPPING
.B touch
.I <kit_dir>/stop
ends launching cleanly: sessions already running finish, and nothing new
starts.
Both
.B drive
and
.B drain
honour it, and neither removes it.
A stop file written before the driver started is ignored, and the driver's
log says so once: one left from an earlier run would otherwise end a new run
before it started anything.
To stop that run, touch it again.
.PP
The ceiling is live.
A driver writes its starting
.B \-\-max
to
.I <kit_dir>/max
and reads it again on every loop, so editing the number lowers or raises it
without a stop; the log records each change.
0 holds every new start.
Sessions already running are never stopped by it.
The file is removed when the driver ends.
.PP
One driver may run per
.IR kit_dir :
a second
.B drive
or
.B drain
refuses while the first's process is alive.
The lock dies with its process, so none is left stale.
Children a dead driver started that still run are counted against the next
driver's ceiling.
.SH THREATS
Every session runs unattended, with no one to ask, in a repo the config
lists, with your git and
.BR gh (1)
credentials, and may push to the mainline.
What can steer such a session:
.IP \(bu 2
The repo's own instruction files
.RI ( CLAUDE.md ,
skills, commands), and its
.IR .claude/settings.json ,
whose hooks run code at the session's start.
Anyone who can push to the repo writes these.
.IP \(bu 2
Text written by people outside it: pull request and issue bodies and
comments, commit messages, CI logs and fetched web pages that the prompt has
the session read.
.IP \(bu 2
What the adopter hands it: the config's
.B vars
and the jobs file's fields, the queue's picks, and the brief files a pass
runs.
.PP
What bounds it is only this: the permission mode and tool rules in
.B claude_args
and the config's
.BR settings ;
the deny and ask rules and hooks kept from the user-level settings; the
credentials the user running ccfleet holds; and the private-name guard,
which checks only the names listed.
A worktree is not a sandbox, and the gate bounds load, not actions.
.PP
The config is the whole grant except for these, which widen it:
.IP \(bu 2
The repo's own
.I .claude/settings.json
and
.IR .claude/settings.local.json ,
which load with its
.I CLAUDE.md
(Claude Code ties the two to one setting source).
Each launch prints a
.B WIDENED
line for any allow rule, default mode, extra directory or approving hook
they hold.
.IP \(bu 2
A kept user-level hook whose script answers allow.
ccfleet drops a hook only when its command line shows the allow; name any
other in
.BR drop_hooks .
.IP \(bu 2
Managed (policy) settings, which always load.
.PP
Leaving the user source out narrows a session in other ways too: user-level
skills, agents and commands under
.I ~/.claude
are not loaded, and
.IR CLAUDE.md 's
@-imports are not followed.
Put what a session needs in a plugin (the kept
.B enabledPlugins
loads it) or in the repo.
.PP
So: use the narrowest permission mode that does the work; never run a bypass
mode against a repo whose instruction files, pull requests or CI output
someone you do not trust can write; give the running user credentials
scoped to these repos; and read each result before trusting what it says it
did.
.B check
names any argument in
.B claude_args
that widens what a session may do.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a config, then the machine's headroom:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccfleet \-\-config fleet.json check
ccfleet \-\-config fleet.json gate
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a run would launch, starting nothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccfleet \-\-config fleet.json run example\-app \e
    \-\-prompt task.md \-\-set TASK=@file:task.txt \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Drive a jobs file, two at once, one per repo:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccfleet \-\-config fleet.json \-\-by 1edeadf7 drive jobs.tsv \e
    \-\-max 2 \-\-serial\-per\-repo
.fi
.RE
.PP
Drain the review queue, then end it early:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccfleet \-\-config fleet.json drain
touch ~/fleet\-kit/stop
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command ran.
For
.B run
and
.BR pass ,
the session's own verdict is in its
.B END
line and result file, not in the exit code.
.B gate
exits 0 for go.
.TP
.B 1
Setup failed: a repo that is not a git checkout, a git command that refused, a
brief missing from the branch (a pass), a bad
.BR \-\-in ,
a user-level settings file that cannot be read (launching without it would
drop its guards), another driver already running, or (for
.BR drain )
a queue not ranked, a queue
.B next
that failed, which is never read as an empty queue, or every pick ending
before its session started; and, for
.BR plan ,
a queue command that failed or printed something other than the JSON it
should.
.B gate
exits 1 for hold.
.TP
.B 2
Claim refused: another session holds the item, or another resume of the
session is running, or the session to resume still runs.
For
.BR pass ,
.B resume
and
.BR "run \-\-item" ,
the queue refused the release; the worktree is kept.
.TP
.B 3
The reset time has been reached; nothing was started.
.TP
.B 4
.B drain
found the queue empty and started nothing.
.TP
.B 5
Refused by the private-name guard, or a placeholder was left unfilled
(for
.BR check ,
a template has one).
.TP
.B 64
Usage: no config, a config that cannot be read, is not JSON, misses a
required key or has a malformed value
.RB ( reset_utc ,
.BR claude_args ,
.BR session_timeout_minutes ),
a malformed or repeated jobs line, a repo given as a path, a
.B \-\-set
with no
.BR = ,
a negative
.BR \-\-max ,
or
.BR drain ,
.BR pass ,
.B plan
or
.B resume \-\-item
without the config they need
.RB ( queue.evidence
for plan,
.B queue.take
and
.B queue.release
for pass,
.B queue.release
for resume).
.PP
A mistake in the command line itself is caught by the argument parser first,
which exits 2.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CCFLEET_CONFIG
The config path, when
.B \-\-config
is not given.
.TP
.B CCFLEET_BY
The default for
.BR \-\-by .
.TP
.B CCFLEET_RESULTS_DAY
Set by
.B drive
for its children: the day folder a
.B run
writes its result to.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Where session transcripts are looked for; default
.IR ~/.claude .
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS
Set to 0 for every session ccfleet starts; see
.BR DESCRIPTION .
.SH NOTES
Sessions started this way take real time and real budget.
.BR run ,
.BR drive ,
.BR drain ,
.B pass
and
.B resume
all launch them; only
.BR check ,
.B gate
and
.B \-\-dry\-run
do not.
.PP
Workers run their own tests niced and single-process, and a prompt should say
so: parallel test runs inside headless sessions have taken a 16 GB laptop past
a load of 100.
.PP
Never give a worker a repo's work as an Agent subagent of the orchestrator:
that is the billing mistake this tool exists to avoid.
.PP
Requires Python 3.8 or later and
.BR git (1);
.BR gh (1)
is used only for a repo whose visibility the config does not state.
The
.B /atelier:fleet sweep
and
.B /atelier:review run
skills drive this tool, and
.B /atelier:review plan
reads the queue through
.BR "ccfleet plan" , and
.I tools/modelguard.py
is a hook that blocks a launch that names no model.
